WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.710
Lesson three.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:09.584
NEAT starts every network as small as possible, and lets mutation make it bigger, one careful step at a time.

00:00:10.184 --> 00:00:14.617
Each new genome starts as a copy of a parent, and then it mutates.

00:00:14.617 --> 00:00:17.433
There are three kinds of mutation.

00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:22.393
The most common one simply nudges a weight, or the bias of a node.

00:00:22.393 --> 00:00:27.107
In our setup, each weight has an eighty percent chance of a small nudge.

00:00:27.107 --> 00:00:31.473
Here, a positive connection drifts until it becomes negative.

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:37.358
The second kind adds a connection, joining two nodes that were not connected before.

00:00:37.358 --> 00:00:39.655
It gets a new innovation number.

00:00:39.655 --> 00:00:42.770
Half of all new genomes get one of these.

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:45.207
The third kind adds a node.

00:00:45.207 --> 00:00:47.883
It picks a connection and splits it in two.

00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:51.608
The old gene is switched off, and two new genes take its place.

00:00:51.608 --> 00:00:56.855
The connection into the new node gets a weight of one, and the one out keeps the old weight.

00:00:56.855 --> 00:00:59.846
So the network behaves almost the same as before.

00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:03.519
The structure has grown, without breaking what already worked.

00:01:03.519 --> 00:01:07.912
Connections and nodes can also be deleted, so networks can shrink too.

00:01:08.512 --> 00:01:10.986
So what happened in the real run?

00:01:10.986 --> 00:01:13.883
Here is the champion of each generation.

00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:20.881
Every run starts fully connected, with all eight inputs wired to all four outputs, and no hidden nodes.

00:01:21.481 --> 00:01:26.357
For its first hundred and sixty generations, evolution mostly removed connections.

00:01:26.357 --> 00:01:34.864
The winner, from generation one hundred and sixty-four, has no hidden nodes at all, and just seventeen of the thirty-two connections it started with.

00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:39.795
Later, the population grew much bigger networks, with fifteen or more hidden nodes.

00:01:39.795 --> 00:01:42.991
But those flew worse on unseen flights, not better.

00:01:42.991 --> 00:01:48.818
A bigger network is not automatically a better one, and that is exactly why NEAT starts small.
